# Hypothesis — how age influences the likelihood of dissaving (NLSY79, 1989 cross-section)

**Headline (conditional hypothesis).** In this sample, the probability that a respondent dissaved in 1989
(*DISSAVED* = 1, withdrawing more from savings than putting in) **falls with age — about −0.5 percentage
points per year, roughly −4.3 points from age 24 to age 32 — and the decline is essentially linear, not
U-shaped; but the gradient is not a general age effect: it is concentrated entirely in the subgroup whose
INCOME takes the constant value 47.279 (inferred to be mean-imputed missing income), while respondents with
genuinely reported income show no detectable age gradient at all.** Any statement about age and dissaving in
this file must therefore be made conditional on that group.

**Direction and magnitude (primary specification: full sample n = 12,686, INCOME_MEANIMPUTED indicator as a
covariate; covariates GENDER, log(1+INCOME), BLACK, HISPANIC).** Marginal rate of dissaving 23.6%. Per year of
age the adjusted odds ratio is 0.969 (95% CI 0.951–0.987); the average marginal effect is −0.53 pp/year.
Predicted probability at covariate means falls from 24.0% at age 24 to 19.7% at age 32, a change of
−4.28 pp (delta-method 95% CI −6.81 to −1.75 pp; nonparametric bootstrap 95% CI −6.59 to −1.49 pp).

**Shape — the quadratic is not supported.** The centred AGE_2 term is insignificant once covariates are in
(coef −0.004, p = 0.35; LR vs the linear-age model = 0.89, df 1, p = 0.35; AIC prefers linear 13109.3 vs
13110.4). Its implied turning point is age 24.0 (95% CI 15.9–32.1), i.e. pinned at the left edge of the
24–32 support and unidentifiable beyond it, so the curvature must be described as interior-only at best, not
as a peak. A factor-in-age specification is no better than the quadratic (LR = 4.30, df 6, p = 0.64). Only in
the raw, unadjusted data is there weak evidence of concavity (LR = 4.76, p = 0.029; maximum at 25.5 years,
CI 23.3–27.8) and it disappears with covariates and completely in the drop-sample. **Defensible language:
dissaving declines with age across the mid-20s to early 30s; there is no reliable evidence of an inverted-U
or U-shape in the adjusted model.**

**Why the effect is conditional (decisive robustness).** Dropping the 3,963 rows with INCOME = 47.279
(31.2%; their value equals the mean of all other rows exactly, the signature of mean-imputed missing income —
an inference, not documented) leaves n = 8,723 and the age effect vanishes: age coefficients jointly
insignificant (LR = 0.18, df 2, p = 0.91), predicted 28.9% at 24 vs 28.6% at 32, change −0.26 pp
(95% CI −3.66 to +3.14). The age × income-group interaction is decisive (LR = 40.8, df 1, p = 1.7e-10):
per-year logit slope −0.0016 (p = 0.88) among genuine-income respondents versus −0.155 (OR 0.856/year,
p < 1e-11) in the imputed group, whose predicted rate falls 16.5% → 5.1% (−11.4 pp) from 24 to 32.
Composition accounts for 56% of the raw −4.00 pp gradient (the imputed-income share rises from 27.1% at ages
24–25 to 38.9% at 30–32 while their dissaving rate falls from 15.6% to 7.0%); the composition-standardised
gradient is only −1.76 pp.

**Mechanism (descriptive, three mutually exclusive savings states).** The decline in dissaving with age is
mostly people moving into positive saving rather than into "no savings": net savers rise 30.9% → 32.0% →
39.0% → 49.5% and "savings unchanged / no savings" fall 44.4% → 42.0% → 37.4% → 29.8% across the age bins
24–25, 26–27, 28–29, 30–32. Because *DISSAVED* and *SAMESAVE* never equal 1 together, DISSAVED = 0 is a
mixture of "flat/no savings" and "net saver", and the age gradient is driven by the second component.

**Covariate associations (adjusted, primary model).** log(1+INCOME): +0.33 per log unit (OR 1.39, p ≈ 1e-39;
AME +5.6 pp) once the imputed-income indicator is held fixed — note the unadjusted income–dissaving relation
is non-monotone (terciles 23.5 / 28.7 / 18.5%). INCOME_MEANIMPUTED: OR 0.248, AME −23.7 pp. Male: OR 0.864
(AME −2.5 pp). BLACK: OR 1.42 (+5.9 pp). HISPANIC: OR 1.16 (+2.4 pp). Age × gender interactions are not
significant (p = 0.79 and 0.72).

**Structural relations to carry forward.** AGE ↔ AGE_2 is an exact functional dependency (AGE_2 = AGE²,
r = 0.999), so it carries no independent information; it was reparameterised as centred AGE and AGE² (VIFs
1.03 and 1.01). DISSAVED ↔ SAMESAVE are mutually exclusive indicators of one three-state outcome
(dissave 23.6% / same-or-none 37.7% / net save 38.7%).

**Scope and caveats.** Single 1989 cross-section of one NLSY79 cohort restricted to ages 24–32 (3% of rows at
age 32): age cannot be separated from cohort or life stage, and nothing here extrapolates outside 24–32 — in
particular this says nothing about dissaving in later life. All estimates are associational: no marriage,
children, education, employment, wealth or debt controls exist in the file, so the coefficients are not causal
behavioural effects. The interpretation of the 47.279 income mass as mean-imputed missingness is an inference
from exact arithmetic and undocumented; the conditional form of the hypothesis is precisely what protects it
from that uncertainty. INCOME units are unverified, 131 exact zeros are taken as real zeros, and the 167 rows
at the maximum (1057.448) look top-coded but winsorising is vacuous because the 99th percentile equals the
maximum, hence the log1p transform. BMI was excluded from the primary model by design (implausible tails, up
to 176 kg/m², and out of scope for this question).
